I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the provided PXRD workflow/tooling first. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, refine them together in GSAS-II, validate the physical models, and export the requested project, CIFs, profile, JSON, and report.

The chemistry narrows the search to Zn–W–O phases and residual ZnO/WO₃, but the pattern must decide whether that simple inventory is sufficient. I’m now checking the frozen reference index and the supplied GSAS-II automation so candidate structures can be compared and refined in a common multiphase state.

The dominant phase is the wolframite framework: its low-angle line at 18.99° and the 30.53/30.77° doublet match an FeWO₄ P2/c analogue after Fe→Zn substitution and lattice contraction. Coherent extra peaks at 23.24° and 23.73° support residual WO₃; weaker zincite lines are also plausible. I’m constructing these models and will test the WO₃ polymorphs and ZnO only in the shared refinement, as required.

The first common-state trials show ZnO’s scale collapsing to numerical zero, so I’m holding its unconstrained cell fixed while leaving its scale active; this prevents a zero-information cell refinement from destabilizing the fit. I’m not yet treating ZnO as absent—the collapse must remain stable after the target and WO₃ models are fully optimized.

The WO₃ screen is discriminating cleanly: the full-cell monoclinic room-temperature references (COD 1528915/2311041) are substantially better than the high-pressure, orthorhombic, and reduced-cell alternatives. The two best references are equivalent settings of the same framework, so I’ll retain the better physical coordinate model and continue refining that shared state.

The first refinements exposed an important replay issue: parameters clipped to physical bounds had left stale calculated arrays in some trial files. A fresh recalculation gives the honest current-state Rwp, so I’m rebuilding the best two-phase state with staged, bounded refinements and an explicit zero-cycle replay after every stage. This is the state that will be eligible for the final project.

The fresh common-state replacements strongly favor monoclinic WO₃ COD 2311041 (Rwp 6.96%) over the other completed alternatives (7.76–17.60%). The triclinic trial has not completed its second refinement and will remain scientifically “unknown” if it cannot finish; I will not count an interrupted run as a rejection.

The transferred wolframite model is now physically sensible and the coordinate refinement lowered fresh Rwp from 15.80% to 7.13%; refining the retained WO₃ framework brought it to 6.95%. Zn–O, W–O, and O–O contacts pass the stated screens. I’m now testing the generator’s remaining discrete line-shape possibilities (uniaxial microstrain axes) in common-state branches before locking the final nuisance model.

I’ve narrowed the model to wolframite ZnWO₄ plus monoclinic WO₃, with ZnO repeatedly collapsing to zero scale. I’m now doing the clean two-phase replay, physical checks, and final export so every submitted file comes from the same saved GSAS-II state.

The clean replay improved substantially after removing the zero-scale ZnO phase: the full-pattern Rwp has fallen from 6.46% to about 5.49% through ordinary two-phase refinement. I’m letting the last coupled atom/profile stages finish, then I’ll reject any numerically better state if its geometry is not chemically credible.

The lowest numerical state fails the physical screen for WO₃ (a 1.52 Å W–O contact), so I’m not submitting it. I’m rolling back to the last chemically credible WO₃ coordinates and will re-optimize only the cells, intensity, texture, and line-shape terms around that structure; this keeps the phase assignment while avoiding an overfit oxygen model.

The physically screened replay is now stable at Rwp 5.58% over all 8,301 measured points. It gives 80.10 wt% ZnWO₄ and 19.90 wt% WO₃; I’m running the same read-only verifier used by the framework to catch any project/CIF/fraction mismatch or negative-background/profile issue before writing the report.

A residual audit changed the phase inventory: the positive residuals at 31.84°, 34.48°, 47.56°, 56.61°, 62.82°, and 67.92° form the zincite ZnO fingerprint. I’m therefore reopening the precursor test in the current two-phase state—holding the accepted phases and nuisance parameters fixed—because the earlier zero-scale result is no longer sufficient evidence to exclude ZnO.

The current-state precursor test is decisive: ZnO refines to a positive ~4–5 wt% contribution and drops full-grid Rwp from 5.58% to 3.66% with its characteristic peaks accounted for. I’m retaining it and now resuming ordinary three-phase refinement from that accepted common state, while keeping the physically screened WO₃ coordinates fixed.

The three-phase model now fits at Rwp 3.04%, and the remaining residuals are mostly positive/negative shoulders around already modeled peaks rather than a new line set. Zincite texture tests stayed essentially random and changed Rwp by only ~0.002%, so I’m treating that as unsupported; I’m doing one last anisotropic-broadening check for this nanocrystalline minor phase before the final replay.

The final three-phase project passes the framework’s read-only replay with no profile review flags, and the exported curve matches that replay numerically. The refined residual ZnO and WO₃ amounts are also equimolar to within ~0.7%, exactly what incomplete ZnO + WO₃ → ZnWO₄ conversion predicts; I’m recording the structural caveats and this mass-balance evidence in the report now.

Completed and validated with a fresh GSAS-II forward replay.

